[#:seon.task{:name "refusal-data-recognizes-errors-by-at-and-layer"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.test-support/refusal-data` (test/seon/test_support.clj:739) recognizes a returned error only when it carries an inst `:seon.error/at`, a qualified `:seon.error/layer` and a qualified `:seon.error/operation`; 102 test sites call it. The errors spec drops :at and :layer from the produced map (errors.md §1, decision 6; slice S2 or with S6 per the S2 re-slicing in tmp/errors/s1-s2-notes.md), after which every returned error reads as committed and those tests silently assert the wrong branch. Done when refusal-data recognizes an error by `:seon.error/type` (the one error? predicate) in the same commit that drops :at/:layer, and a test-support test covers a typed error without :at. Proof: a new seon.test-support-test/refusal-data-recognizes-a-typed-error (to be written first) naming this task."
             :must-read #{seon.test-support/refusal-data}
             :evidence #{"docs/seon/specs/errors.md" "docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/task-census/evidence/errors--s1-s2-notes.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "evaluate-answers-nil-for-a-refused-form",
            :kind :function,
            :intent
            "(`seon.test-support/evaluate` {`:seon.cluster.eval/source` \"#:seon.repl{:value 1}\" ...}) whose only form the batch refuses (:seon.batch/refused :fabricated-response) answers nil: the last entry has no `:seon.batch/evaluation`, so the function's own :map output contract refuses with a contract error instead of the refusal (seen by the reader-prose lane's forgive_test on a check JVM, 2026-10-07). Correct: the refused entry's error reaches the caller as data, as evaluate-row builds an unrun evaluation for it in the turn.",
            :must-read
            #{seon.turn/evaluate-row seon.test-support/evaluate}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-test-member-connection-is-not-the-cluster-head"
             :kind :namespace
             :intent "A test member's connection is its own branch, not the cluster head, so a test of a function that compares a branch with its cluster head (RF-1, 24e8cc2df: `my.program/write-branch!`, `my.program/diff`) must build its own cluster-<name> branch by hand. Correct: one test-support helper gives a member a cluster head and a branch off it, and the clojure-testing skill names it. Proof: a seon.test-support-test deftest (to be written) asserting the helper's branch compares against the head it made."
             :evidence #{"docs/seon/schedule/cards/RF-1.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "file-backed-branch-without-republication"
             :kind :function
             :intent "with-published-file-database publishes the whole checkout into an empty store for every test (refresh-source! onto a fresh root), so each blob-staging test pays a full publication: seon.web.jvm-test runs 38-55 s per test in the throwaway check JVM (2026-10-08, SP-3 web half), against 3 tests in 3018 ms on with-database, whose in-memory store refuses staging (seon.blob/stage-file!: no process root). Correct: a file-backed fixture branch with the program projection that does not republish the checkout per test, e.g. one published file root per source commit branched per test, or a file-backed fresh store carrying the installed schema and projection. Proof: seon.web.jvm-test/oversized-bodies-spill-byte-exactly-through-the-blob-tier under the 5 s bound with the namespace's :seon.test/long removed."
             :must-read #{seon.test-support/with-published-file-database seon.test-support/populate-published-root! seon.blob/stage-binary!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-08/storage-point/sp3-web-handover.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "assign-task-drops-its-refusal"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.test-support/assign-task!` returns nil whatever `seon.task/create!` and `seon.task/assign!` answer, so a refused assignment is silent. Given an agent's branch connection, it declares its proof on that branch while `seon.task/assign!` reads the proofs at main (`seon.task/main`), refuses :no-proofs, and the test continues unassigned (lane a1-home, 2026-10-08: the first draft of `seon.turn-work-test/a-branch-agent-assigned-after-its-fork-opens-its-model-turn` failed for this reason). Correct: the helper declares the proof where the task lives and fails the test on any refusal value. Done when a call that `seon.task/assign!` refuses fails loudly at the helper."
             :must-read #{seon.test-support/assign-task! seon.test-support/declare-proof! seon.task/assign! seon.task/main-connection}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-running-agent-world-has-no-task-home"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.test-support/with-running-agent` stands up a cluster whose root row names no `:seon.agent/branch`, so `seon.task/main` answers nil there: a task assigned to an agent hosted on its own branch (as `seon.mcp` hosts a new agent id) is written on the member's branch after the agent forked, the agent reads none and never opens a model turn. A running cluster's root row names its own branch (default: :cluster-default). `seon.competency-grade-test/a-launch-grades-each-run` writes the row itself (`home-root!`). Done when the running-agent world carries root's home row as a booted cluster does and that test's local helper is deleted."
             :must-read #{seon.test-support/with-running-agent seon.task/main seon.cluster.host/home-call}}
 #:seon.task{:name "the-bench-writes-trees-and-the-reconciler-launches"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Card D1. Delete `seon.test-support/own-branch-agent!`'s setup evaluation and `seon.test-support/outside-wake-on-branch!` (the \"Work your task.\" wake): the bench writes a subject with step tasks and the reconciler launches them; the bench test is green through that path."
             :must-read #{seon.test-support/own-branch-agent! seon.test-support/outside-wake-on-branch!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/2026-10-11/runtime-follows-db.md"}}]
